# ComposeUX Render Engine & Composer — Build Roadmap [PROJECT:CXC-ROADMAP]

> How to get the "correct" model working: a renderer-agnostic ComposeUX where a widget is defined once and renders pixel-identically on web, desktop, and CYD, with Composer itself rebuilt on that engine. Written as a first-pass build brief. The normative architecture is [SPEC:CXC-RENDER]; this document only sequences its construction.

---

## 1. Intent — the End-State

### [CXC-RM-ENDSTATE] What "Correct" Means

- definition: ComposeUX is the three-layer render engine of [CXC-R-ARCH] — declarative `.compose.yaml` widgets over a platform-independent engine (layout, hit-test, focus, text, animation, assets) over thin per-platform drawing backends. A widget is authored once and renders pixel-identically on desktop, browser, and CYD. Composer is rebuilt on this engine (ultimately a browser app), previews *real* engine-rendered widgets, and stays bus-native and Intelligence-drivable.
- key-insight: CTk is not the foundation — it is the interim runtime the engine is designed to replace ([CXC-R-PHASES]: "Phase 1 replaces CTk widget rendering"). The destination is toolkit-agnostic; CTk retires to the Python/Skia dev-reference role.
- status: planned
- part-of: [PROJECT:CXC-ROADMAP]

### [CXC-RM-RUNTIME-DIST] Widget Runtime Distribution (where deployed widgets run)

- clarification: This is **runtime-location frequency** — where deployed ComposeUX widgets actually execute in production — NOT development order. By frequency: (1) **macOS**, (2) **Raspberry Pi**, (3) **Browser**. CYD is a low-volume POC tier; mobile is out of scope. (This ranks where *widgets* run; where the *Composer tool* runs is a separate question — see [CXC-RM-M4].)
- key-insight: The two highest-frequency runtimes collapse into ONE backend — macOS and RPi both run **Python + Skia** (RPi is a capable Linux/ARM SBC, not a constrained MCU like the CYD). So the workhorse backend, carrying the bulk of production widget execution, is Python-Skia; the browser is the lowest-frequency of the three.
- consequence-invest: Backend hardening/perf effort follows frequency — Python-Skia (macOS+RPi) is the workhorse and earns the most investment; the browser backend serves fewer sessions, which relaxes its perf bar and argues against letting it drive the architecture.
- consequence-decision1: Because the high-frequency runtimes are Python and mobile is out of scope, keep the engine Python and do NOT adopt Flutter (it would demote the workhorse to reference-only to serve the least-frequent target). Decision 1 narrows to Python-in-browser vs a browser-only re-port — see [CXC-RM-D1].
- consequence-dev-order: Development order is set by the critical path ([CXC-RM-PATH]), not by this frequency — but they align: the M0–M2 Python trunk happens to build the two highest-frequency runtimes first; the browser (lowest frequency) is M3.
- implication-rpi: Linux/aarch64; fullscreen/kiosk (DRM-KMS or Wayland/X11), possibly rotated. May need skia-python built for aarch64 or a GL surface — a bounded M0 risk. RPi can run the config-driven fungible-display boot ([CXC-R-BOOT]) as a Python app — the middle tier between macOS and CYD.
- status: planned
- part-of: [PROJECT:CXC-ROADMAP]

---

## 2. Current State (measured 2026-07-06)

### [CXC-RM-STATE] Two Parallel Worlds; the Hard Bet Is Already Proven

- key-insight: The architectural risk is retired. The same widget code renders pixel-comparable on **desktop Skia** and a **resource-starved ESP32/CYD** (ILI9341, 7.6 KB band buffer), verified by a working pixel-diff QA harness. "One tiny contract, render anywhere" is demonstrated, not aspirational.
- production-world: `ux_composer/src/composeio_renderers/` — ~11 widgets, each a `CTkFrame` subclass, wired into the dashboard + preview. **Load-bearing. CTk-coupled by design.**
- engine-world: `python/composeio/render/` — the `Canvas` contract (ABC) + a real Skia raster backend + `cyd/` C++ backend + 5 agnostic widgets (gauge/led/label/rect/circle). **Not wired into any app.**
- gaps-that-define-the-work:
  - Skia backend is raster-only — `present()` is a no-op; **no live window / event loop**.
  - **No Layer 2 engine** (layout, hit-test, focus, text, gesture, animation) in the agnostic world.
  - **No `.compose.yaml` runtime** — widgets are constructed programmatically/statically, not parsed from config.
  - Only 5 of ~16 widgets exist on the contract; the 11 production widgets are CTk-only.
  - **No browser backend** — the web target is spec + an SSE bus-bridge (`web.py`), zero renderer.
  - CYD backend implements a *subset* of the contract (no fill_arc/paths/transforms/draw_image/opacity) and has no config-fetch boot.
- status: operational (as described)
- see-also: [CXC-RM-ENDSTATE]
- part-of: [PROJECT:CXC-ROADMAP]

