### [CXC-RM-BEHAVIORS] Operational (declarative layer) — State-Driven Behaviors
- goal: A declarative, HA-informed behavior/binding layer in `.compose.yaml`: (a) bindable widget attributes — `visible`, and per David 2026-07-06 also **transforms** `rotation` (deg), `opacity`, `scale` — bound to a namespace; (b) **inline variables** (declare once, reuse/bind; parse-time substitution); (c) state-driven **temporal behaviors** (blink, pulse, pulse-opacity, ramp-color, animate/transition) triggered by a namespace condition.
- principle: **state on the bus, behavior in the widget** — the bus carries semantic state (`red_alert = on`, published once), the widget owns the temporal presentation (blink at 2s); frames are never streamed over the bus. Same separation as ComposeBus state engines, extended to presentation. Engine-owned → consistent + pixel-verified across every backend; Intelligence-drivable at intent altitude (flip one bit).
- seed: Button `led_behaviors` + `engine/animation.py` prove the pattern; generalize to a config-level mechanism attachable to any widget/attribute. Bindable rotation is trivial on the Skia tier (`canvas.rotate`); costly on CYD (raster bitmap rotation) — note, not a blocker.
- reference: Home Assistant's trigger→condition→action model + its inline `variables:`.
- decision-inline-code (2026-07-06, David): **NO arbitrary inline Python in `.compose.yaml`.** It breaks define-once (Python can't run on the CYD/C++ or a TS-browser backend) and is an RCE surface on a fetch-from-bus display fleet. ESPHome's inline-C++ works only because it targets ONE compiled runtime; ComposeUX is define-once-render-many. Escape hatch for real logic = a **bus workload** (compute → publish a namespace → widget binds it) — compute where it belongs, config stays declarative + portable.
- dsl-deferred (2026-07-06, David): a **sandboxed, restricted expression DSL** (HA-Jinja-flavored: arithmetic, `ns()` lookups, conditionals — interpretable identically on Python AND CYD/JS) is the sanctioned middle ground, but it is its **own scope + deliverable** and is **use-case-gated** — collect ~5 real high-need use cases before speccing, because a DSL is testing-heavy and we minimize test scope. Start with the declarative 90%.
- dsl-gate-MET (2026-07-07): the use-case gate is now **satisfied** — Headless Widgets Pass 1 ([CXC-RM-HEADLESS]) harvested **6** real high-need use-cases from building the jump-drive triad declaratively (logged `~/.composeio/dsl_candidates.jsonl`, source `headless-pass1`): (1) variable-rate integration per tick (`remaining -= rate·dt`); (2) conditional rate arithmetic over a subscribed value; (3) multi-namespace boolean condition (AND) in one trigger — the flagship, which cost 3 widgets + 2 namespaces + 2 bus hops to fake declaratively; (4) open-ended comparison operators in triggers (no `>=`, forced a `1e9` sentinel); (5) canon-aware/typed value-map lookup (bool `True` misses the `"on"` key the `is:` canonicalizer would match — divergent matching rules); (6) derived/scaled output values (`fuel_pct = remaining/capacity*100`). These 6 are the Pass-2 DSL scope — the DSL is now **un-gated on use-cases**, gated only on being scheduled as its own deliverable.
- spec: the declarative layer is specified in **[SPEC:CXC-BIND]** (`16-composeux-bindings-behaviors.lts`, 2026-07-06); the Composer binding editor follows it.
- status: declarative layer (a/b/c) = **OPERATIONAL 2026-07-06** — [SPEC:CXC-BIND] compliance C1–C8 all PASS (29 tests + 5 goldens, `tests/render/test_bind_behaviors.py`); suite 232 green macOS + RPi5; sampled behavior frames SHA-256-identical macOS↔RPi5 (deterministic `render_frame_at` sampling); Intelligence-over-real-MQTT smoke 4/4 (`smoke_bind_intelligence_mqtt.py`); demos `render/examples/red_alert.compose.yaml` + `dradis.compose.yaml`. C++ contract gained translate/rotate/scale/set_opacity declarations w/ ESP32 no-op stubs (see the [CXC-R-DRAW] change record — flagged for David). Expression DSL = still deferred, use-case-gated (0 of ~5 gating use-cases collected so far — nothing in this pass needed it).
- part-of: [PROJECT:CXC-ROADMAP]

### [CXC-RM-LOTTIE] Planned — Lottie Animation Widget (own pass)
- goal: A LottieWidget realizing the specified-but-unbuilt Lottie capability ([CXC-R-DRAW-LOTTIE], [CXC-R-C17]/[CXC-R-C18]) — vector animation for non-interactive motion (e.g. the dradis sweep).
- fit: renders via **skottie** (Skia's own Lottie module) on the Skia tier (macOS/RPi/MinnowBoard) — pixel-consistent by construction; **skottie ships in CanvasKit**, so the browser gets it nearly free IF Decision 1 goes Pyodide+CanvasKit (a further point for Option A); on CYD, degrade to a **pre-rendered sprite sheet** baked from the same Lottie ([CXC-R-C18]).
- bus-bindable playback (David 2026-07-06): bind namespaces/variables to `loop`, `autoplay`, `goToAndStop(frame)`, `playSegments(a,b)`, `setSpeed` — "state on the bus, behavior in the widget" applied to animation. Dradis sweep = a Lottie whose speed/segment is namespace-bound.
- spec: **[SPEC:CXC-LOTTIE]** (`17-composeux-lottie.lts`, 2026-07-06) — build-ready.
- status: Skia tier = **OPERATIONAL 2026-07-07** — C1–C5, C7 PASS (25 compliance tests `tests/render/test_lottie.py`; suites 276 macOS / 272+4skip RPi5; 8/8 sampled dradis frames **SHA-256-identical** macOS↔RPi5; Intelligence-over-MQTT smoke 5/5 BOTH platforms; live window 58fps w/ mid-run bound-speed change). C6 (CYD sprite sheet) DELIVERED in M5 2026-07-07: `scripts/bake_lottie_sprite.py` bakes any Lottie through this same evaluator into CI16 frames at `bus://assets/animations/<name>/`, and the CYD SpriteWidget plays them (dradis sweep verified on hardware). **Skottie verdict: skia-python (m144) does NOT bind skottie (issue #57 open since 2020, no symbols in either platform wheel)** — built instead on a zero-dep Canvas-contract Lottie-subset evaluator (`render/lottie/`, renderer seam documented; python-lottie rejected on AGPLv3+), pending David's ratification — see the [SPEC:CXC-LOTTIE] change records, incl. the stroked-cubic-contour cross-build divergence finding (fills bit-exact; annulus-fill authoring rule; a pinned self-built Skia would fix strokes AND unlock skottie in one investment).
- part-of: [PROJECT:CXC-ROADMAP]

### [CXC-RM-GESTURE] Planned — Single-Touch Swipe Gestures
- goal: Extend the interaction engine ([CXC-R-ENGINE-TOUCH]) with **4-way directional swipe** (up/down/left/right) on a **single touch point** (no multitouch/pinch needed, David 2026-07-06). Tap/long-press/pan already exist (M1).
- bus-native: a swipe publishes an interaction event to a namespace, so an Intelligence can "swipe" identically to a finger; matters for touch displays (CYD, touch monitors on RPi/Minnow).
